> Well, 22 is EINVAL on Linux and 'man select' says that select() returns EINVAL
> when n is negative, so somehow the maximum filedescriptor fed to select() manages
> to become negative.  According to the http://www.afterstep.org/code, afterstep
> has two functions that start with 'WaitFor', namely 'WaitForExpose()' and
> 'WaitForButtonsUp()'.  However, the source code to the X server has a function called
> 'WaitForSomething()' in 'xc/programs/Xserver/os/WaitFor.c' on line 342,
> where 'MAXSOCKS' is the culprit.
> And in 'xc/programs/Xserver/os/osdep.h', around line 70,
> we find a piece of code that can easily lead to MAXSOCKS being -1
> 
> #ifndef OPEN_MAX
> #ifdef SVR4
> #define OPEN_MAX 256
> #else
> #include <sys/param.h>
> #ifndef OPEN_MAX
> #if defined(NOFILE) && !defined(NOFILES_MAX)
> #define OPEN_MAX NOFILE
> #else
> #define OPEN_MAX NOFILES_MAX
> #endif
> #endif
> #endif
> #endif
> 
> #if OPEN_MAX <= 256
> #define MAXSOCKS (OPEN_MAX - 1)
> #else
> #define MAXSOCKS 256
> #endif
> 
> I think.  But I'm not sure; just a quick source code scan.
> 
> KC

In short; when compiling for your system,
maybe somebody forgot to #define NOFILE _and_ NOFILES_MAX.
If that's the case (and I'm not very sure about this), write to the person who
packages these rpms, or the person who's supposed to have created the standard
header files for your Linux distribution (Suse, that is); because in my
Linux, OPEN_MAX is already #define-d in /usr/include/linux/limits.h as 256.
Of course it's just a possibility; this needs to be proven by recompiling
the Xserver for your system with some sort of echo of this value.

you can also add the lines

#if !defined(OPEN_MAX) && !defined(SVR4) && !defined(NOFILE) && !defined(NOFILES_MAX)
#error "impossible system configuration"
#endif

on top of all these defines inside the source code and recompile.


