daniel <shenxueyu@yahoo.com> writes:

>   An I/O-bound program is one that, if run alone, would spend more time 
> waiting for I/O than using the processor. A processor-bound program is the 
> opposite. Suppose a short-term scheduling algorithm favors those programs 
> that have used little processor time in the recent past. Could u tell me 
> why this algorithm favors I/O-bound programs and yet does not permanently 
> deny processor time to processor-bound programs.

Ah, mid May, end of semester looming is it?

It does not. There could be enought IO bound processes to a) stay
within the high-pri window, and b) use 100% between them. Thus there
will always be a high priority IO completion process, and your CPU
bound process will not be scheduled. See the VMS sysgen docs for the
ugly details of stopping this from being a major problem if the lo pri
process is holding an 'interesting' lock or in a critical section. The
last is called priority inversion.

