generic.s, avanti.s, miata.s), but none of them will boot.  They hang on
'calibrating delay loop'.  A trawl through the web reveals that this is
possibly caused by the real time clock (RTC) being munged by the ARC (BIOS
emulation
thingy for Windows NT installation).  As I understand it, you can't boot the
bootdisk from the
ARC although you can set the RTC, but as soon as you drop down into the
'SRM' bios prompt the RTC format is different from that in ARC, but stored
in the same place, therefore it gets messed up.  To make matters worse, you
used to be able to set the RTC from the SRM prompt, but Digital decided to
remove it to save space.  Arrgh!

Ahem.  I have downloaded an ISO image called 'Jumpstart', which I think is
based on RatHead - this kernel will boot  into a fairly silly initialization
program and give you a root shell but so far I've not managed to fool it
into loading the 'color1.gz' and 'color2.img' disks that go with Slack.
I've tried (amongst other things);

>>> boot dka500 -fi boot/vmlinuz -fl "rw root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1"

Which prompts for the root disk, says it's found a compressed image, then
panics with 'can't find root fs' type messages.  Probably because this
particular kernel doesn't support minix filesystem images - tries to load
MSDOS ones - doh!

What I need is a kernel image without RTC support but with Minix fs support.

??

