The upgrade instructions mention running quite some programs on Tru64 
to create that srm-bootable upgrade floppy, but my perception is that 
using dd to write the image to the disk on linux *should* be enough. 

If not (e.g. if you srm tells you that the disk is not bootable), you 
can find a DOS/Windows system, get mkboot.exe from 
ftp://ftp.digital.comU/pub/Digital/Alpha/firmware/utilities/mkboot.exe 
and follow the instructions from mkboot.txt (same ftp directory) to 
create the disk. Then again, use boot dva0 in srm to boot it.

Or you can mail me and I'll create a proper boot floppy image for you 
on a Tru64 box.

 PW> I'm getting a bit fed up with Alpha.  I can see why Sun outsells
 PW> everyone now.  I'd REALLY like to be able to get this Alpha to 
 PW> work though, it is cheap and interesting but VERY frustrating.  

It just depends on what you are accustomed to. As for me, I am 
regularly frustrated by Sparc/Solaris boxes because I just don't know 
even the basics of SysV'ish Unix or Sun's hardware, while I am very 
accustomed to BSD'ish systems like the default in Tru64, and also to 
the SRM console and Alpha hardware ...

 PW> I don't think I'll be buying that 533a as I was thinking about.  

Hey. You have bougth an AXPpci33. Besides being fucking slow, this is 
ages old and was an OEM board, and building it together was intended to 
be done by OEM system integrators only. This is not just like plugging 
a PC together - you have to find the *right* PCI cards and you even 
have to find the *right* case plus cooler (unless you were so lucky to 
get a version with a cooler PLUS fan preinstalled). You have to read 
manuals to understand the hardware and the srm console etc., because 
back when the axppci was built, the srm did not yet feature anything 
like help features. 

If you buy an 533au (really, get one with a *u* if you can), you will 
get a completely mounted system with fitting hardware (a good scsi 
board, a good graphics board which interactly nicely with the system 
mainboard) and with an up-to-date srm (implementing all the help 
features etc. - the 533au has a much larger eeprom so the console can 
be much more comfortable as compared to the axppci) which will cause 
you dimensions less of trouble as compared to an axppci board.

