Hello,
Ryan Abernathey wrote:
> recognizes my eccentric hard drive, a Mylex DAC960 raid array.  I have
	This will be a bit troublesome. IIRC the linux driver for the mylex
cards needs a mylex bios in the 3.x range whereas digital only supplied
cards with a OEM-bios in the 2.x range. The work around seems to be to
update the adapter bios to version 3 and then use the linux driver. The
are at least two catches I am aware of though. First you need two flash
chips for V3, if your card does not sport two prom-sockets you might as
well can the project. And if it sports two sockets then you can start to
hunt down the probable rare chips. The second catch is alpha specific.
The adapter bios utility to setup the raid groups  will not operate in
an alpha box. The work around seems to be to put the card in an x86
machine, do all configuring there and drop it back into the alpha. The
third (but in comparison minor) problem is the fact that your alpha
won#t boot from the mylex...
> 
> When I boot from the "Jumpstart CD," one of the kernel messages at
> startup is:
> sym53c8xx: device not recognized
> I suspect this is the raid controller?
	Nope, this is the symbios driver for the onboard scsi controller. It
probably gives something like ncr53c8xx: device recognized before or
after this message. Newer symbios based controllers can do tricks the
old ones can not. This is just the kernels way of letting you know.
	
Ahoi
	Sebastian Moeller


